A||||4||x-thumpr-x||won't start on own battery (power drain)||||1||
Z||000000||x-thumpr-x||10-22-2001||04:45 PM||||O.K.  got a little problem and unsure what it is? My 85GT will not start unless it gets a boost. The battery holds a charge at 12.67V. When running, the charge is 14.4V (is this high for this car?). We tried starting the motor up on it's own battery and I noticed the voltage dropped to 7.5V on trying to start (used a meter). This is not right!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <BR>I tried this on my 84 coupe, upon starting, the voltage dropped from 12.6 to 11.8V. <BR>Hmmmmmm. what could be drawing all the power? When the car ignition left in the run position, it reads a steady 12.6V, no power drain. So, I've come to the theory that the starter might be drawing to much voltage or something else. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P> Any help on solving this little problem would greatly be appreciated. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Need the car to be able to start up on it's own power! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><BR> (also, this battery will start my other 85GT no problem)<P>------------------<BR> Where there's a Willie, there's a way!<BR>red 84 coupe<BR>white 85GT<BR>black 85GT <BR>Willie..||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-22-2001||06:16 PM||||Battery old? Maybe it is bad.<P>Starter cooked and drawing way too many amps to crank. That takes care of the jump start part. (suspected)||||reg||1
Z||000002||DrDave||10-22-2001||09:57 PM||||You probably already know, but some don't, when you check the battery voltage you Do care if you measur at the Post or on the connector.  When you start your car you are drawing the most current.  If your posts/battery connectors are corroded, then Your symptoms could easily occur.  I even had one where the factory crimp on the battery connector gave up.  Looked great. but when you measured the voltage at the connector it was 7.8 volts.  When ya measured at the post it was 12.3 volts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gary W||10-22-2001||10:04 PM||||Check the battery cables at *both* ends. Also, there should be a ground strap or two on the engine block for a return current path. All must be free of corrosion.||||reg||1
Z||000004||x-thumpr-x||10-22-2001||10:48 PM||||Thanks for the advice. Looks like I'll be under the car again this saturday. Oh boy!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">  Not as easy as my 4 banger I found out last weekend. Little more cluttered!  I hope it's just a bad connection on the engine. $100 for a rebuilt starter (that's with the core trade). I know this well since my 6000 wagon had the same starter. Was able to swamp a few new parts from the 6000 to my 84 fiero (rebuilt starter and altenator).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
